Both are single sentences, each well under 200 characters. Entry A pitches a bottled-smell product for insomniacs; Entry B pitches hired mourners performing at an ex's wedding, with an explicit price per tear.

**Reason against the house criteria, in order:**

Criterion 1: both pitch a bizarre startup idea that sounds ridiculous yet could be a real business — no difference here. Criterion 2 is the first where they differ. Entry A's core — a scent product that aids sleep via memory — is a familiar business shape (aromatherapy, scent-and-memory products) with a nostalgic twist bolted on; it is charming but a reader has roughly heard of this category. Entry B's premise — professional mourners repurposed from funeral traditions into breakup theatre at an ex's wedding — is a genuinely stranger recombination, something the reader has not encountered in that form. Criterion 3 also favors B: it makes the willingness to pay explicit and concrete in its own words ("heartbroken clients pay $500 a tear"), whereas A only asserts "will pay anything" without grounding the market. Criterion 4: both land as one-sentence pitches with a turn, roughly even; B's turn ("the tragedy it deserved") goes from laugh to nod slightly more sharply. Criterion 5: both are concrete and name the thing sold and the buyer; A names the product and insomniacs, B names the service, the occasion, the buyer, and the price. Since criterion 2 is the first differing criterion and B wins it clearly, with criterion 3 reinforcing rather than overturning, B decides.

Verdict: B
